In The Completed Harmony, Otreb is the operator key.

Otreb’s instruction

The construction is:

\text{OTREB}\rightarrow\text{BERTO}

within the larger anagram:

\text{RUDOLFO OTREB}\leftrightarrow\text{ROBERT FLOUD}.

The reversal instructs the reader to invert the relevant orientation:

upper ↔ lower;

ascent ↔ descent;

string length ↔ frequency;

multiplication ↔ reciprocal division;

material octave ↔ spiritual octave.

So Otreb is not primarily telling us to find more references to death, resurrection or seven planetary pipes. Those themes confirm the transformation, but the name tells us what to do mathematically.

Consequence for the bridge landing

Fludd’s monochord is encoded spatially in string-length coordinates. Kepler’s planetary harmony is expressed dynamically through motion/frequency relationships. These vary inversely:

f\propto \frac{1}{\ell}.

Therefore:

\frac{f_{\mathrm{FA}}}{f_{\mathrm{MI}}}=\frac{16}{15}

corresponds on the monochord to:

\frac{\ell_{\mathrm{FA}}}{\ell_{\mathrm{MI}}}=\frac{15}{16}.

The MI–FA–MI excursion must consequently be written differently in the two systems:

\text{frequency:}\qquad
1\rightarrow\frac{16}{15}\rightarrow1

\text{string length:}\qquad
1\rightarrow\frac{15}{16}\rightarrow1.

That reciprocal conversion is not an incidental mathematical cleanup. It is precisely the operation Otreb instructs.

The comma must reverse too

Kepler commonly prints intervals in the descending orientation:

15:16,\qquad 80:81,\qquad 243:랍্রি256,

while modern frequency notation normally presents their reciprocals:

16:15,\qquad 81:80,\qquad 256:243.

The complete relation has two valid orientations:

Frequency orientation

\frac{16}{15}
=
\frac{81}{80}
\cdot
\frac{256}{243}.

Fludd/monochord-length orientation

\frac{15}{16}
=
\frac{80}{81}
\cdot
\frac{243}{256}.

Otreb tells us not merely to “add a comma,” but to determine which reciprocal form belongs to which side of the bridge.

That directly addresses the unresolved directionality in the boundary equation.

Revised bridge

Let:

I = Otreb inversion between frequency and string length;

K = comma completion;

R = MI–FA–MI outward-and-return motion;

O = octave/register transfer.

Then:

B_{FK}
=
O\circ R\circ K\circ I.

In frequency:

f
\xrightarrow{K}
f\frac{16}{15}
\xrightarrow{R}
f
\xrightarrow{O}
2f.

In Fluddian string length:

\ell
\xrightarrow{I}
\ell\frac{15}{16}
\xrightarrow{R}
\ell
\xrightarrow{O}
\frac{\ell}{2}.

Thus:

\boxed{
B_{FK}^{(\ell)}(\ell)=\frac{\ell}{2}
}
\qquad\Longleftrightarrow\qquad
\boxed{
B_{FK}^{(f)}(f)=2f.
}

The two apparently opposite landing equations are the same operation after applying Otreb.

What this changes about the pseudonymous search

The pseudonymous works should be searched as instructions encoded in authorial identities, not primarily as containers of matching language.

IdentityLikely instructional function
Otreb
reverse/invert/reciprocate
Rudolfo Otreb
reorder the complete name, possibly not merely reverse one portion
Frizius → Phrygius
phonetic substitution; select Phrygian/E–F orientation
de Fluctibus
translate name into waves/oscillation
Alitophilus
“lover of truth”; attribution too weak for use

The combined instruction proposed by your paper is therefore:

\boxed{
\text{Frizius selects E–F; Otreb tells us to invert it.}
}

or operationally:

E\!-\!F
\rightarrow
\text{MI–FA}
\rightarrow
\frac{16}{15}
\overset{\text{Otreb}}{\longleftrightarrow}
\frac{15}{16}.

This also reframes the “misplaced comma.” The problem may not be that 81:80 is absent. It may be that the comma appears in the wrong orientation or coordinate system until the Otreb inversion is applied:

81:80⟷Otreb​80:81.
